  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTonight more showers and storms will be moving across central IL. \nThe main concern is the flash flooding risk. WPC has a small area of \neastern IL in a moderate risk (level 3 of 4) and near and north of I-\n72 in a slight risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall for the \novernight hours (valid until 12z). The 00z sounding shows the \nenvironment having PWATs of 2.2-2.4 inches again. Flooding is \nalready ongoing in many places, left over from last night's event. \nIt will not take much to exacerbate the situation overnight if the \nnew convection decides to set up over that stripe of central IL \n(Fulton Co to Vermilion Co) again. LPMM is suggesting that we could \nsee another localized stripe of 4-8 inches somewhere in the area \nnorth of I-72. \n\nThere is a slight risk (level 2 of 5) south of I-70 and a marginal \nrisk for areas east of the IL River Valley for damaging winds again \ntoday. The NAMNest is depicting a more widely dry, nearly no \nactivity, solution. The HRRR has an area of convection developing \nlate afternoon in west central IL, progressing eastward into the \novernight hours with the cold front. After how much rain north of I-\n70 has received over the last several days, the hope is for the \ndrier solution, but plan for the stormier solution. With the HRRR \nversion of events, it is showing pockets of 2-4 inches of rainfall \nfrom 21z-09z today into tomorrow. The good news though would be that \nif this activity were to develop, it would be fairly progressive and \nless of a training set up, unlike previous days. \n\nTemperatures remain hot and humid for one more day. The heat \nadvisory for south of I-70 is valid through 8pm (01z) this evening. \nHeat indices of 100-105 degrees are expected.  \n\nA brief break in the active weather and the heat is looking likely \nfor Monday into Tuesday. A high pressure center will set up over the \nUpper Mississippi Valley / Great Lakes region behind a cold front \nthat will move through central IL during the day today. Temperatures \nstarting Monday into much of the next week will be mainly in the \nupper 70s to 80s for highs and 60s to low 70s for lows.\n\nMore showers and thunderstorm re-enter the forecast by Tuesday \nevening into Wednesday. SPC has central IL in a marginal risk for \nsevere weather for Tuesday west of I-55, which would be for the late \nday convection. There are additional chance for rain through \nThursday before returning to a mostly dry forecast into the \nlate week.",
